#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166629 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f04980f8-35db-5cf4-8a4d-8e9f0f23fb1e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "b6135ab8-d329-5646-aeb7-dea8b38e7606" :question/math-academy-id "e-8420" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}$\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle \\int _{-1}^{1} \\coth{x}\\sinh{x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{\\sin{x}}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\coth{x}=\\dfrac{\\cosh{x}}{\\sinh{x}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-{e}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-(-1)}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2e-2{e}^{-1}}{2} \\\\  & =e-{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166630 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e14a3673-7b90-5f81-b73a-c2894f07c561" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "555cf939-5320-5230-bcef-4828210fbc34" :question/math-academy-id "e-8421" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\tanh}{\\mathop{\\rm tanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{\\tanh^2{x}}{2\\sinh^2 x} \\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\tanh}{\\mathop{\\rm tanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$First, we write our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{\\tanh^2{x}}{2\\sinh^2 x}\\textrm{d}x =\n\t\t\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}}\\cdot \\tanh^2{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\tan{x}=\\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{\\cos{x}}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is  \n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{\\sinh{x}}{\\cosh{x}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {tanh}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{sech}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{e-{e}^{-1}}{e+{e}^{-1}}-\\frac{{e}^{-1}-e}{{e}^{-1}+e}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{e}^{2}+1}-\\frac{1-{e}^{2}}{1+{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{e}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{e}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166631 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7fafc0a-8fff-5c9d-a55a-f25ce3c1d729" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Hyperbolic Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "27ef207b-1337-5a3b-92cc-0d7a8b6bb13e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8422" :question/problem "Evaluate \n$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\ln 3} \\tanh x   \\, \\textrm{d}x  }.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\tan{x} = \\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{\\sinh{x}}{\\cosh{x}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u = \\cosh{x}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sinh{x} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\textrm{d}u = \\sinh{x} \\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration according to the rule $u = \\cosh{x}.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\ln 3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{5}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5/3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{1}^{5/3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166725 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c7ddaa7-f225-56f3-966a-f214152b1ad0" :knowledge-point/title "Deriving a Hyperbolic Identity Using Osborn's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "e05932ad-3ee7-5b8a-a0c9-8e8ac50990d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9881" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n       \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}$Find a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity $1 + \\cot^2{x} = \\csc^2{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n       \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}$Osborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2{x} = \\csc^2{x}\n$$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\cot^2{x} = \\dfrac{\\cos^2{x}}{\\sin^2{x}} \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\csc^2{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2{x}}\n$$\n\nand thus, we have two products of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n1 \\,{\\mathbf{\\color{red}{-}}}\\, \\coth^2{x} = {\\mathbf{\\color{red}{-}}}\\,\\csch^2{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c7ddaa7-f225-56f3-966a-f214152b1ad0" :knowledge-point/title "Deriving a Hyperbolic Identity Using Osborn's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e07aea-260c-5921-8de8-d04c22d40c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536299} {:answer/id #uuid "26d2fb87-73ae-5a6b-90bf-69f713e47020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)=2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536343} {:answer/id #uuid "b28508db-0cd8-544d-999a-08776558bf64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)=-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536570} {:answer/id #uuid "d25733c2-3d33-5bb1-aafb-e34cc2667a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536615} {:answer/id #uuid "e8748be8-b203-589d-aaf9-5ee1d5c46030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d25733c2-3d33-5bb1-aafb-e34cc2667a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536615} :answer-field/id #uuid "22b72122-c08b-57d4-b94a-a03d93b80384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "826a3dfa-4e58-52b9-b4cc-53a7b2b826d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-138949" :question/problem "$$\nFind a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "$$\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a product of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y\\,-\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c7ddaa7-f225-56f3-966a-f214152b1ad0" :knowledge-point/title "Deriving a Hyperbolic Identity Using Osborn's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d9e97b8-e512-516e-8ae6-4de402f6b85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x-y)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536412} {:answer/id #uuid "b73ad957-5c90-5f33-8162-155e4e01158a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536574} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b713d6-928a-5284-9165-2435ed51e0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536637} {:answer/id #uuid "e9664b47-5d9b-5cc8-92fa-11c86b5da539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536648} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb9280d-9adf-5c2f-8f47-855cf75173a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdb9280d-9adf-5c2f-8f47-855cf75173a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536690} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e0e726d-6993-5071-a69e-aca3d0e40b18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21d74430-9169-5f5c-9745-dbb6cae86953" :question/math-academy-id "q-138950" :question/problem "$$\nFind a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).$" :question/worked-solution "$$\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a product of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+y) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7fafc0a-8fff-5c9d-a55a-f25ce3c1d729" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Hyperbolic Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "575e949f-a2b7-5302-ade7-234c593749f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{175}{288}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536436} {:answer/id #uuid "84f09ef6-af8c-5278-868c-878e38fd43f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{175}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536501} {:answer/id #uuid "94da350d-d600-5d09-a5e8-a507d6bea83a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{288}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536520} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8049a3-094e-56a7-a9bb-cd14ccc9b2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536540} {:answer/id #uuid "e4be4b9f-6f9d-51ae-b85e-355d75cde324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "575e949f-a2b7-5302-ade7-234c593749f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{175}{288}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536436} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e082909-a6e2-55c2-a4a7-4878e06b14ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "390d417d-9926-5446-a159-d0e16ff521f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-138954" :question/problem "$$\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}{u}^{-3}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{32}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{175}{288}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c7ddaa7-f225-56f3-966a-f214152b1ad0" :knowledge-point/title "Deriving a Hyperbolic Identity Using Osborn's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a54b6a7-f164-57c2-9d18-a35fbbab2114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)={cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536301} {:answer/id #uuid "36744821-b10b-57d0-9a41-509c0394e6b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536368} {:answer/id #uuid "49555273-5842-5a42-a1d3-66dd471f73a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)={sinh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536399} {:answer/id #uuid "4b57663a-c099-5e3f-b686-05fdeb0b2b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536404} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c639d3-b514-565a-aef9-5928de4ef0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36744821-b10b-57d0-9a41-509c0394e6b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536368} :answer-field/id #uuid "df7e218a-51b1-5a2c-9dfe-061597bffce9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c62464eb-da81-57d3-9709-e91e0d4b8042" :question/math-academy-id "q-139016" :question/problem "$$\nFind a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "$$\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not have a product of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f04980f8-35db-5cf4-8a4d-8e9f0f23fb1e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a24661b-9376-5478-ba22-345b80fbea58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a24661b-9376-5478-ba22-345b80fbea58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536531} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a779e2-c85d-5324-8778-80dd2097d44f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c94b3bf4-462a-5a97-a5df-84bb2aaaa4fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-346474" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{coth}^{2}⁡x{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identities corresponding to the trigonometric identities \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{coth}^{2}⁡x{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}-{e}^{-0}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-{3}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{1-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{3})}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f04980f8-35db-5cf4-8a4d-8e9f0f23fb1e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66c4c321-c3fc-50a9-83a8-59c121c7db0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66c4c321-c3fc-50a9-83a8-59c121c7db0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536459} :answer-field/id #uuid "371769d0-a8d2-5429-b519-c67818b5ff8d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd25bce0-e06f-5534-8414-1587d88ad138" :question/math-academy-id "q-352673" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identities corresponding to the trigonometric identities \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}-{e}^{-0}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-{2}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{1-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f04980f8-35db-5cf4-8a4d-8e9f0f23fb1e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f737d4-4208-55a3-aa88-7222aded4409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25f737d4-4208-55a3-aa88-7222aded4409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536340} :answer-field/id #uuid "c87e6d3b-23bd-5b1e-bec0-bba379741512" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b13f329b-704f-5b15-801d-b08d9d90b1ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-352678" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nFirst, we recall the following  identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3-{3}^{-1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f04980f8-35db-5cf4-8a4d-8e9f0f23fb1e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56ae9a87-b6bd-553c-894c-fb7b9c46e795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56ae9a87-b6bd-553c-894c-fb7b9c46e795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536434} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1b5624b-d189-5839-a384-dfb6d3457d12" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4fc19ee-fc09-58f6-b456-25b6d7c144cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-352684" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{sech}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{sech}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x})}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2-{2}^{-1}}{2+{2}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{2}}{2+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\frac{5}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e14a3673-7b90-5f81-b73a-c2894f07c561" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36b11b91-37c1-568c-9d1a-8a39694f67a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36b11b91-37c1-568c-9d1a-8a39694f67a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536369} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eb4c32a-3d20-50c8-8f9e-4bb89fd9ad7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee29c156-d06d-572b-b229-09cda1e3a6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-352915" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{2{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{2{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {coth}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {coth}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{csch}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e14a3673-7b90-5f81-b73a-c2894f07c561" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fcdb4ad-14d5-5f0f-bf9e-41acc5a1d22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fcdb4ad-14d5-5f0f-bf9e-41acc5a1d22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536329} :answer-field/id #uuid "241e61f4-57a8-5907-834c-7042fc78b039" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fa8b923-c944-5ab9-8f5d-a280f219b3bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-352916" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}{2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}{2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {tanh}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {tanh}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{sech}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e14a3673-7b90-5f81-b73a-c2894f07c561" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98919bb4-2785-53dd-9466-f9d0288a76cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98919bb4-2785-53dd-9466-f9d0288a76cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536528} :answer-field/id #uuid "408af374-202e-561f-90a6-1910a956b51e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f16bf74-fd62-5389-9de5-031fc7e1da5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-352935" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we find the hyperbolic identities corresponding to the trigonometric identities \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identities to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{sech}^{2}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{sech}^{2}⁡(0)+\\frac{1}{2}{sech}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{4}{({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2})^{2}}-\\frac{4}{({e}^{0}+{e}^{0})^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(1/2+2)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{50}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7fafc0a-8fff-5c9d-a55a-f25ce3c1d729" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Hyperbolic Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036a64a0-313e-560e-a52e-dc6b8481224c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{104}{255}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "036a64a0-313e-560e-a52e-dc6b8481224c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{104}{255}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536289} :answer-field/id #uuid "59c3d70b-5195-5774-847f-b546cbff6162" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d070173-586a-5be7-bd5c-a4be4b18eeea" :question/math-academy-id "q-352966" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}2\\operatorname{csch}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{coth}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}2\\operatorname{csch}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{coth}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{coth}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡16$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{coth}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡16}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(u)\\operatorname{coth}⁡(u)\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(u){|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡16} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡16)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡16)}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{16-{16}^{-1}}{2})}+\\frac{1}{(\\frac{4-{4}^{-1}}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{255}{32})}+\\frac{1}{(\\frac{15}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{104}{255}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7fafc0a-8fff-5c9d-a55a-f25ce3c1d729" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Hyperbolic Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2b669f-2579-5335-819c-e77eab944787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a2b669f-2579-5335-819c-e77eab944787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536346} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af736d1-f000-5f15-b738-443b7520d5c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f7758d7e-a5ba-50ee-a45d-ff68b3cc6226" :question/math-academy-id "q-352968" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{csch}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{csch}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{csch}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot {sinh}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5/3}u\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{5/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{25}{9}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f04980f8-35db-5cf4-8a4d-8e9f0f23fb1e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8abbc701-fd57-5e8f-a04a-2e363418c822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165609} {:answer/id #uuid "a3027c11-4f32-54eb-af22-c8c6f931ed2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165610} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e7dc8a-e303-5ede-9956-feb0245b7a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165611} {:answer/id #uuid "f650e3cb-270a-5d6c-b194-a6df6b50eaf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165612} {:answer/id #uuid "4c67467a-de32-5061-b17a-5a68199c51b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f650e3cb-270a-5d6c-b194-a6df6b50eaf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165612} :answer-field/id #uuid "b66d5234-ac6b-50a2-bf9b-30debd44462c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "37030af8-6f60-5f87-96ef-c720c85ba743" :question/math-academy-id "q-95742" :question/problem "$$\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "$$\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}+{e}^{-0}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+{2}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{1+1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e14a3673-7b90-5f81-b73a-c2894f07c561" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037ba1f6-bab8-5644-a9f9-1d0997895160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+3\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536290} {:answer/id #uuid "05a68fca-8c3b-59b9-b009-99d530c3e80c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sech}⁡x-3\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536293} {:answer/id #uuid "3058b8fa-e6f3-593c-9d60-424339d2e4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536356} {:answer/id #uuid "44b1da90-8ca0-5d57-b272-29370ec5c888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536387} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8cb1aa-d2c5-5da0-96f0-51596aafbb34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{csch}⁡x-3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3058b8fa-e6f3-593c-9d60-424339d2e4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536356} :answer-field/id #uuid "33e72fa2-cc65-5b80-8195-b5d8928f38d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15a967e4-1f67-5afa-8031-c76c4fbb09cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-96263" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}(5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to express the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡x(5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡x(5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e14a3673-7b90-5f81-b73a-c2894f07c561" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "234cc714-ee95-51d3-9f60-96338562378e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536333} {:answer/id #uuid "4f31d1d3-9617-5495-b1c8-0bd332072ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536419} {:answer/id #uuid "529a7029-8e13-5a97-9918-a3b488d2c78c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536424} {:answer/id #uuid "70329641-1279-5076-9f37-fb06772be638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536471} {:answer/id #uuid "fffb787f-ff79-5c83-b983-661bbcea077a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "234cc714-ee95-51d3-9f60-96338562378e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536333} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f4c241c-0bbe-5dbc-9897-1ee7bd328728" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb7adc98-109e-57e5-9f19-72ef71c8b698" :question/math-academy-id "q-96265" :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\,\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identities to express the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}+\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4-{4}^{-1}}+\\frac{2}{2-{2}^{-1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c7ddaa7-f225-56f3-966a-f214152b1ad0" :knowledge-point/title "Deriving a Hyperbolic Identity Using Osborn's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44e24fff-6edb-58dd-b6c8-7731cb263263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536388} {:answer/id #uuid "454d23bc-df8f-5fb0-9ac4-bbc97aefba45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536390} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3e4413-d9ee-532c-ae52-619bdac2ce6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536415} {:answer/id #uuid "5985a81f-c958-57c9-9d4b-af828a400130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536442} {:answer/id #uuid "9c651855-a335-5bd9-bf70-edb22dff84f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44e24fff-6edb-58dd-b6c8-7731cb263263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536388} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbdfdfa3-9b3a-546a-977e-1d072d1016bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09b00f02-3f58-5541-80d9-f11db2b14004" :question/math-academy-id "q-9628" :question/problem "$$\nFind a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "$$\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a product of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x\\,-\\,{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c7ddaa7-f225-56f3-966a-f214152b1ad0" :knowledge-point/title "Deriving a Hyperbolic Identity Using Osborn's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fb53bbb-85a3-5bf3-a469-97f9b6d515c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x={sinh}^{2}⁡x+{cosh}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536355} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9358f5-ef1f-54b1-99ef-1197f59d64d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536420} {:answer/id #uuid "81cbace4-5806-5b41-b4de-0e5c8bcb22b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536495} {:answer/id #uuid "81e96895-4bc5-5473-8e80-cdb7431151cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536496} {:answer/id #uuid "c9355724-e50d-561b-a5bc-0428aabebe3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x={sinh}^{2}⁡x-{cosh}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81cbace4-5806-5b41-b4de-0e5c8bcb22b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536495} :answer-field/id #uuid "5299e7d7-dd38-52d1-9c80-440f5a957600" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3747ab66-800e-539f-851f-023604b67b37" :question/math-academy-id "q-9631" :question/problem "$$\nFind a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not have a product of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7fafc0a-8fff-5c9d-a55a-f25ce3c1d729" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Hyperbolic Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3076c98e-4b5f-5295-a960-def6c68868a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{csch}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536357} {:answer/id #uuid "790e9e20-c3f7-5319-bd5c-6ff1a8086450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{sech}^{4}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536485} {:answer/id #uuid "951bf2e0-4ba8-5e1e-9dc3-e53cb361313b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{csch}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536521} {:answer/id #uuid "d05d1a37-cbd0-5beb-a2c1-20973dce818c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}{sech}^{4}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536613} {:answer/id #uuid "ec738ce0-bbfd-546f-8829-537d42455730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{sech}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec738ce0-bbfd-546f-8829-537d42455730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{sech}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536658} :answer-field/id #uuid "d71d4fa5-c4c0-5c1e-bcc7-f6ccd2a9c51a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8879f5ec-f2c2-51de-a1ef-edc8baad4b56" :question/math-academy-id "q-96498" :question/problem "$\\int {sech}^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nFirst, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity and rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sech}^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{4}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{4}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{cosh}^{3}⁡x}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{sech}^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7fafc0a-8fff-5c9d-a55a-f25ce3c1d729" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Hyperbolic Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371ed2b5-3cfd-5f36-ad25-78277503bf11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536371} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6e5545-ad9f-555a-83ad-2208b3d8fb3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536405} {:answer/id #uuid "57ae105c-8d7c-5650-bab1-27e1f5494f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536437} {:answer/id #uuid "62f42da6-b448-5558-a669-efbc711b9a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536451} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7c2265-44f1-5e96-9940-d8c787667e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{8}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b6e5545-ad9f-555a-83ad-2208b3d8fb3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536405} :answer-field/id #uuid "4002135c-7d30-54e3-97cb-3c714ed2683f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b4fa5f6e-c8a3-5731-a2a2-6fc871183cc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-97309" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
